  planner_response {
    response: "An update was received for task: a8be0d2c-b06c-4918-b266-9068b9b84c8d/task-63\nTask output:\n{\"text\":\"The derivative of \\\\( x^2 \\\\) with respect to \\\\( x \\\\) is **\\\\( 2x \\\\)**.\\n\\nHere is a quick breakdown of why:\\n\\n### The Power Rule\\nThe easiest way to find this is using the **Power Rule** for derivatives, which states:\\n\\n$$\\\\frac{d}{dx}[x^n] = n \\\\cdot x^{n-1}$$\\n\\n### Step-by-Step\\n1. Identify the power: Here, \\\\( n = 2 \\\\).\\n2. Bring the power to the front: \\\\( 2 \\\\cdot x \\\\).\\n3. Subtract 1 from the exponent: \\\\( 2 - 1 = 1 \\\\).\\n4. Put it together: \\\\( 2x^1 = \\\\mathbf{2x} \\\\).\\n\\n---\\n\\n### The Intuition: What does this actually mean?\\nA derivative simply tells you the **instantaneous rate of change** or the **slope of the tangent line** at any point on the curve \\\\( y = x^2 \\\\).\\n\\n* If you want to know how steep the parabola \\\\( y = x^2 \\\\) is at \\\\( x = 1 \\\\), plug in 1: the slope is \\\\( 2(1) = 2 \\\\).\\n* At \\\\( x = 0 \\\\) (the very bottom of the curve), the slope is \\\\( 2(0) = 0 \\\\) (a flat, horizontal tangent).\\n* At \\\\( x = -3 \\\\), the slope is \\\\( 2(-3) = -6 \\\\) (steeply sloping downwards).\",\"rawText\":\"The derivative of \\\\( x^2 \\\\) with respect to \\\\( x \\\\) is **\\\\( 2x \\\\)**.\\n\\nHere is a quick breakdown of why:\\n\\n### The Power Rule\\nThe easiest way to find this is using the **Power Rule** for derivatives, which states:\\n\\n$$\\\\frac{d}{dx}[x^n] = n \\\\cdot x^{n-1}$$\\n\\n### Step-by-Step\\n1. Identify the power: Here, \\\\( n = 2 \\\\).\\n2. Bring the power to the front: \\\\( 2 \\\\cdot x \\\\).\\n3. Subtract 1 from the exponent: \\\\( 2 - 1 = 1 \\\\).\\n4. Put it together: \\\\( 2x^1 = \\\\mathbf{2x} \\\\).\\n\\n---\\n\\n### The Intuition: What does this actually mean?\\nA derivative simply tells you the **instantaneous rate of change** or the **slope of the tangent line** at any point on the curve \\\\( y = x^2 \\\\).\\n\\n* If you want to know how steep the parabola \\\\( y = x^2 \\\\) is at \\\\( x = 1 \\\\), plug in 1: the slope is \\\\( 2(1) = 2 \\\\).\\n* At \\\\( x = 0 \\\\) (the very bottom of the curve), the slope is \\\\( 2(0) = 0 \\\\) (a flat, horizontal tangent).\\n* At \\\\( x = -3 \\\\), the slope is \\\\( 2(-3) = -6 \\\\) (steeply sloping downwards).\\n\\n<<<SUGGESTIONS: [\\\"Can you show how to derive this using the limit definition of a derivative?\\\", \\\"How do I find the derivative of higher powers like x^3 or x^4?\\\", \\\"Quiz me on applying the power rule!\\\"]>>>\",\"suggestions\":[\"Can you show how to derive this using the limit definition of a derivative?\",\"How do I find the derivative of higher powers like x^3 or x^4?\",\"Quiz me on applying the power rule!\"],\"quiz\":null}\nTask exited with return code: 0\nTask duration: 4.887s\nTask completed at: 2026-10-31T04:53:50-07:00.\nTask stdout and stderr are available at: /.aistudio/task-output/a8be0d2c-b06c-4918-b266-9068b9b84c8d/task-63.log\nTask exit code: 0\n\nYou must delete this task using manage_task (action=\'kill\') now that it has completed."
