  user_input {
    user_response: "Fix the AI Tutor\'s error handling in my existing Study Flow application.\n\nCURRENT PROBLEM:\n\nWhen a student asks a question, the AI sometimes returns:\n\n503 UNAVAILABLE: This model is currently experiencing high demand.\n\nThe application displays the raw JSON error directly inside the chat.\n\nIMPLEMENT THESE IMPROVEMENTS:\n\n1. Handle temporary 503 errors properly.\n\n   * Implement automatic retries using exponential backoff.\n   * Use a maximum of 3 retry attempts.\n   * Add small random delays between retries.\n   * Avoid sending duplicate messages or creating duplicate AI responses.\n\n2. Improve the user interface.\n   Instead of displaying raw JSON, show:\n\n   \"Our AI tutor is temporarily busy. Please try again in a moment.\"\n\n   Include a clear Retry button.\n\n3. Preserve the student\'s question.\n   If a request fails, keep the original message in the chat so the student does not have to type it again.\n\n4. Handle other errors separately.\n\n   * 429: Explain that the usage limit has been reached.\n   * 401: Explain that API authentication needs attention.\n   * 503: Explain that the AI service is temporarily unavailable.\n   * Network errors: Explain that the connection failed.\n\n5. Model availability:\n   Inspect the currently configured Gemini model.\n   If it is temporarily overloaded, use a configured alternative model only if it is supported by the current API and project.\n   Do not invent model names or switch to paid services.\n\n6. Preserve all existing features, including conversational memory, diagrams, mathematical formatting, saved chats and Study Roadmap integration.\n\n7. Test the error-handling workflow using simulated 503 and 429 responses. Confirm that failed requests can be retried without duplicating messages.\n\nIMPORTANT:\nDo not merely hide the error. Fix the underlying request-handling logic and display useful messages to students."
    items {
      text: "Fix the AI Tutor\'s error handling in my existing Study Flow application.\n\nCURRENT PROBLEM:\n\nWhen a student asks a question, the AI sometimes returns:\n\n503 UNAVAILABLE: This model is currently experiencing high demand.\n\nThe application displays the raw JSON error directly inside the chat.\n\nIMPLEMENT THESE IMPROVEMENTS:\n\n1. Handle temporary 503 errors properly.\n\n   * Implement automatic retries using exponential backoff.\n   * Use a maximum of 3 retry attempts.\n   * Add small random delays between retries.\n   * Avoid sending duplicate messages or creating duplicate AI responses.\n\n2. Improve the user interface.\n   Instead of displaying raw JSON, show:\n\n   \"Our AI tutor is temporarily busy. Please try again in a moment.\"\n\n   Include a clear Retry button.\n\n3. Preserve the student\'s question.\n   If a request fails, keep the original message in the chat so the student does not have to type it again.\n\n4. Handle other errors separately.\n\n   * 429: Explain that the usage limit has been reached.\n   * 401: Explain that API authentication needs attention.\n   * 503: Explain that the AI service is temporarily unavailable.\n   * Network errors: Explain that the connection failed.\n\n5. Model availability:\n   Inspect the currently configured Gemini model.\n   If it is temporarily overloaded, use a configured alternative model only if it is supported by the current API and project.\n   Do not invent model names or switch to paid services.\n\n6. Preserve all existing features, including conversational memory, diagrams, mathematical formatting, saved chats and Study Roadmap integration.\n\n7. Test the error-handling workflow using simulated 503 and 429 responses. Confirm that failed requests can be retried without duplicating messages.\n\nIMPORTANT:\nDo not merely hide the error. Fix the underlying request-handling logic and display useful messages to students."
    }
  }
}
