  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Defining Error State**\n\nI\'m currently focused on defining the `TutorErrorState` interface within `AITutorView.tsx`. This involves specifying properties like `message`, `errorCode`, `statusCode`, and others to effectively handle and communicate error information. The goal is to create a robust and informative way to present and manage potential issues within the tutoring interface.\n\n\n**Handling Error Conditions**\n\nI\'ve been working on how the AI tutor handles and displays errors. I\'m focusing on extracting error messages and codes from responses, setting an `activeError` state that includes the message, error code, status code, and related details like the pending text and attachment. The student\'s original question is also preserved, even when errors occur, so we ensure the student\'s question is not lost.\n\n\n**Implementing Retry Logic**\n\nI\'m now implementing the `handleRetry` function. My focus is ensuring a clean retry process. It involves checking for existing errors and loading states, clearing the active error, and then initiating a new API call with the original user input, attachment and assessment request. Importantly, I\'m ensuring no duplicate user messages are appended, as the original message is already present in the session history. I\'m also ensuring the original question, attachment, and assessment context are re-sent.\n\n\n**Refining Error Handling**\n\nI\'m refining the error handling logic, catching potential `json()` parsing errors and providing default fallback messages. I\'m also mapping response status codes (503, 429, 401) to specific error codes for better clarity. On successful responses, I\'m parsing the JSON, creating a `ChatMessage` object, and updating the session with the new AI message, including suggestions and quiz data. This session update also incorporates a timestamp for the latest activity.\n\n\n**Handling Retry Failures**\n\nI\'m now focusing on handling errors that occur during the retry process itself, specifically within the `handleRetry` function\'s `catch` block. I\'ve implemented a robust error handling approach. If a retry fails, I\'m setting a default error state, which provides a helpful message and appropriate error codes, incorporating the failed message ID, pending text and attachment, plus the assessment context, for informational purposes. This ensures users are informed even when retries fail. Finally, the loading state is always set to false in the `finally` block to ensure the user knows an action has completed.\n\n\n**Styling Error Indicators**\n\nI\'m now styling the error indicators. I\'m dynamically applying Tailwind classes based on the `errorCode` or `statusCode`. I\'m assigning background, border, and text colors using a ternary operator. I\'m using distinct icons such as `Sparkles`, `Clock`, and `ShieldAlert` for `MODEL_BUSY_503`, `USAGE_LIMIT_429`, and `AUTH_ERROR_401` respectively, to enhance visual feedback for different error scenarios, using the standard alert icon as a catch all for unknown issues.\n\n\n**Enhancing Error Displays**\n\nI\'m now working to provide more informative error displays by including specific details, and dynamically changing content based on the `errorCode` or `statusCode`. I\'m personalizing the error descriptions to provide clarity on the user\'s specific problem. I\'m also ensuring the message includes a status code and any pending user text.\n\n\n**Displaying Error Context**\n\nI\'m now focusing on displaying the preserved question within the error context, ensuring the user sees the original input alongside the error details. I\'m dynamically rendering the preserved question, the pendingText from the `activeError` state, below the main error message, improving clarity for the user, and enabling a clear understanding of the context of the error.\n\n\n"
