    source: CORTEX_STEP_SOURCE_USER_EXPLICIT
    source_trajectory_step_info {
      trajectory_id: "a8be0d2c-b06c-4918-b266-9068b9b84c8d"
      step_index: 182
      metadata_index: 89
      cascade_id: "a8be0d2c-b06c-4918-b266-9068b9b84c8d"
    }
    model_api_content_id: "3a79e2f28ef5426cb7629e01ad841556"
  }
  user_input {
    user_response: "Improve my existing STUDY FLOW application by fixing three important problems.\n\nIMPORTANT:\nInspect the existing code before making changes. Preserve the current design, branding, layout and working features. Do not rebuild the application unnecessarily.\n\n## 1. IMPROVE AI RESPONSE SPEED\n\nPROBLEM:\nThe AI Tutor takes too long to generate answers.\n\nREQUIREMENTS:\n\n* Investigate the actual reason for slow responses.\n* Check whether unnecessary API requests are being made.\n* Prevent duplicate API calls.\n* Avoid sending excessive conversation history with every request.\n* Preserve enough conversation context for meaningful follow-up questions.\n* Use a suitable, fast AI model supported by the current API configuration.\n* Implement streaming responses so answers appear progressively instead of waiting for the entire response.\n* Avoid unnecessary image generation when a simple SVG or CSS diagram would work.\n* Keep explanations accurate and useful. Do not sacrifice quality just to make responses shorter.\n* Show a clean loading indicator while the AI is responding.\n* Handle 429 and 503 errors properly with appropriate retry options.\n* Do not repeatedly retry requests that have exhausted their quota.\n\nThe goal is to make the AI Tutor responsive and reduce unnecessary waiting.\n\nIMPORTANT: Do not claim that all delays can be eliminated. If the AI provider is experiencing high demand, show an honest temporary-unavailability message.\n\n## 2. FIX MATHEMATICAL FORMATTING AND AI RESPONSES\n\nPROBLEM:\nThe AI currently displays raw Markdown and LaTeX syntax.\n\nFor example, it displays:\n\n2\\text{H}_2 + \\text{O}_2 \\longrightarrow 2\\text{H}_2\\text{O}\n\nInstead of displaying a properly formatted chemical equation.\n\nREQUIREMENTS:\n\nA. MARKDOWN FORMATTING\n\nProperly render:\n\n* Headings.\n* Bold and italic text.\n* Bullet points.\n* Numbered lists.\n* Tables.\n* Blockquotes.\n* Code blocks when actual code is being displayed.\n\nNever expose raw Markdown formatting symbols to students when they should be rendered.\n\nB. MATHEMATICAL FORMATTING\n\nImplement a reliable mathematical rendering system such as KaTeX.\n\nSupport:\n\n* Inline equations.\n* Block equations.\n* Fractions.\n* Powers and exponents.\n* Subscripts.\n* Square roots.\n* Greek letters.\n* Mathematical symbols.\n* Chemical equations.\n* Physics formulas.\n* Units and scientific notation.\n\nUse standard mathematical delimiters:\n\n* $...$ for inline equations.\n* \\(...\\) for separate equations.\n\nDo not display mathematical expressions inside code formatting.\n\nExamples of how equations should appear:\n\nPhysics:\n\nF = m \303\227 a\n\nChemistry:\n\n2H\342\202\202 + O\342\202\202 \342\206\222 2H\342\202\202O\n\nMathematics:\n\nx = (-b \302\261 \342\210\232(b\302\262 - 4ac)) / 2a\n\nAlso explain the meaning of symbols underneath equations whenever appropriate.\n\nC. VISUAL PRESENTATION\n\n* Display equations clearly and separately from paragraphs when necessary.\n* Ensure formulas are readable on mobile and desktop.\n* Use proper spacing between explanations and equations.\n* Handle incomplete or invalid mathematical expressions gracefully.\n* Sanitize rendered AI content to prevent unsafe HTML or JavaScript execution.\n* Do not display raw LaTeX commands such as \\text{}, \\longrightarrow or \\frac{}{}.\n\nInspect the existing rendering pipeline and fix the actual problem rather than simply changing the AI prompt.\n\nDo not break the existing chat interface or conversational history.\n\n## 3. REMOVE ALL FAKE AND PRE-POPULATED PROGRESS DATA\n\nPROBLEM:\nThe website currently displays progress statistics and study activities even when a student has not actually completed them.\n\nI want STUDY FLOW to begin with a clean and honest dashboard.\n\nRemove all:\n\n* Fake completed study sessions.\n* Pre-filled study hours.\n* Artificial streaks.\n* Fake assessment scores.\n* Sample completed roadmaps.\n* Invented learning statistics.\n* Fake progress percentages.\n* Artificial recent activities.\n* Hardcoded achievements.\n\nREQUIREMENTS:\n\nA. NEW STUDENT EXPERIENCE\n\nWhen a new student opens Study Flow, display:\n\n* Total study time: 0 hours.\n* Completed sessions: 0.\n* Completed roadmaps: 0.\n* Assessments completed: 0.\n* Learning progress: 0%.\n\nShow friendly empty-state messages such as:\n\n\"No study activity yet. Start your first learning session!\"\n\n\"Your completed roadmaps will appear here.\"\n\n\"Your progress will grow as you study.\"\n\nB. REAL PROGRESS TRACKING\n\nOnly update progress when the student actually performs an action.\n\nExamples:\n\n* A study session counts after it is genuinely completed.\n* A roadmap session counts after the student marks it completed.\n* Assessment statistics update after an assessment is submitted and checked.\n* Study time updates based on actual recorded timer sessions.\n* Streaks update based on real study activity.\n\nC. DATA STORAGE\n\n* Save genuine progress using the existing persistence system.\n* Restore saved progress after refreshing the website.\n* Do not reset existing real student data.\n* Do not overwrite real progress with sample data.\n* Keep different students\' data separate where user accounts are supported.\n\nD. SAMPLE DATA\n\nIf sample or demonstration data is necessary for development, keep it separate from real student accounts.\n\nDo not show sample data in the normal student dashboard.\n\n## 4. FINAL TESTING\n\nAfter making these changes, test:\n\nTEST 1:\nAsk the AI Tutor to explain Newton\'s Second Law. Check whether the answer appears progressively and without unnecessary delays.\n\nTEST 2:\nAsk it to explain the formation of water. Check that the chemical equation is properly formatted.\n\nTEST 3:\nAsk a question involving fractions, powers and square roots. Confirm that the mathematical formatting works.\n\nTEST 4:\nOpen a new student account or a clean dashboard state. Confirm that every progress statistic begins at zero.\n\nTEST 5:\nComplete an actual Pomodoro session and a roadmap session. Confirm that genuine progress is recorded correctly.\n\nTEST 6:\nRefresh the website and confirm that real saved progress remains available.\n\nIMPORTANT FINAL INSTRUCTIONS:\n\n* Preserve all four main features: AI Tutor, Study Roadmap, Paper Analyzer and Focus Tools.\n* Preserve existing saved conversations and genuine student data.\n* Do not replace working features with static mockups.\n* Do not introduce fake responses, fake progress or decorative buttons.\n* Make actual code changes and verify the results.\n* Prioritise reliability, speed, accuracy and a clean user experience."
    items {
      text: "Improve my existing STUDY FLOW application by fixing three important problems.\n\nIMPORTANT:\nInspect the existing code before making changes. Preserve the current design, branding, layout and working features. Do not rebuild the application unnecessarily.\n\n## 1. IMPROVE AI RESPONSE SPEED\n\nPROBLEM:\nThe AI Tutor takes too long to generate answers.\n\nREQUIREMENTS:\n\n* Investigate the actual reason for slow responses.\n* Check whether unnecessary API requests are being made.\n* Prevent duplicate API calls.\n* Avoid sending excessive conversation history with every request.\n* Preserve enough conversation context for meaningful follow-up questions.\n* Use a suitable, fast AI model supported by the current API configuration.\n* Implement streaming responses so answers appear progressively instead of waiting for the entire response.\n* Avoid unnecessary image generation when a simple SVG or CSS diagram would work.\n* Keep explanations accurate and useful. Do not sacrifice quality just to make responses shorter.\n* Show a clean loading indicator while the AI is responding.\n* Handle 429 and 503 errors properly with appropriate retry options.\n* Do not repeatedly retry requests that have exhausted their quota.\n\nThe goal is to make the AI Tutor responsive and reduce unnecessary waiting.\n\nIMPORTANT: Do not claim that all delays can be eliminated. If the AI provider is experiencing high demand, show an honest temporary-unavailability message.\n\n## 2. FIX MATHEMATICAL FORMATTING AND AI RESPONSES\n\nPROBLEM:\nThe AI currently displays raw Markdown and LaTeX syntax.\n\nFor example, it displays:\n\n2\\text{H}_2 + \\text{O}_2 \\longrightarrow 2\\text{H}_2\\text{O}\n\nInstead of displaying a properly formatted chemical equation.\n\nREQUIREMENTS:\n\nA. MARKDOWN FORMATTING\n\nProperly render:\n\n* Headings.\n* Bold and italic text.\n* Bullet points.\n* Numbered lists.\n* Tables.\n* Blockquotes.\n* Code blocks when actual code is being displayed.\n\nNever expose raw Markdown formatting symbols to students when they should be rendered.\n\nB. MATHEMATICAL FORMATTING\n\nImplement a reliable mathematical rendering system such as KaTeX.\n\nSupport:\n\n* Inline equations.\n* Block equations.\n* Fractions.\n* Powers and exponents.\n* Subscripts.\n* Square roots.\n* Greek letters.\n* Mathematical symbols.\n* Chemical equations.\n* Physics formulas.\n* Units and scientific notation.\n\nUse standard mathematical delimiters:\n\n* $...$ for inline equations.\n* \\(...\\) for separate equations.\n\nDo not display mathematical expressions inside code formatting.\n\nExamples of how equations should appear:\n\nPhysics:\n\nF = m \303\227 a\n\nChemistry:\n\n2H\342\202\202 + O\342\202\202 \342\206\222 2H\342\202\202O\n\nMathematics:\n\nx = (-b \302\261 \342\210\232(b\302\262 - 4ac)) / 2a\n\nAlso explain the meaning of symbols underneath equations whenever appropriate.\n\nC. VISUAL PRESENTATION\n\n* Display equations clearly and separately from paragraphs when necessary.\n* Ensure formulas are readable on mobile and desktop.\n* Use proper spacing between explanations and equations.\n* Handle incomplete or invalid mathematical expressions gracefully.\n* Sanitize rendered AI content to prevent unsafe HTML or JavaScript execution.\n* Do not display raw LaTeX commands such as \\text{}, \\longrightarrow or \\frac{}{}.\n\nInspect the existing rendering pipeline and fix the actual problem rather than simply changing the AI prompt.\n\nDo not break the existing chat interface or conversational history.\n\n## 3. REMOVE ALL FAKE AND PRE-POPULATED PROGRESS DATA\n\nPROBLEM:\nThe website currently displays progress statistics and study activities even when a student has not actually completed them.\n\nI want STUDY FLOW to begin with a clean and honest dashboard.\n\nRemove all:\n\n* Fake completed study sessions.\n* Pre-filled study hours.\n* Artificial streaks.\n* Fake assessment scores.\n* Sample completed roadmaps.\n* Invented learning statistics.\n* Fake progress percentages.\n* Artificial recent activities.\n* Hardcoded achievements.\n\nREQUIREMENTS:\n\nA. NEW STUDENT EXPERIENCE\n\nWhen a new student opens Study Flow, display:\n\n* Total study time: 0 hours.\n* Completed sessions: 0.\n* Completed roadmaps: 0.\n* Assessments completed: 0.\n* Learning progress: 0%.\n\nShow friendly empty-state messages such as:\n\n\"No study activity yet. Start your first learning session!\"\n\n\"Your completed roadmaps will appear here.\"\n\n\"Your progress will grow as you study.\"\n\nB. REAL PROGRESS TRACKING\n\nOnly update progress when the student actually performs an action.\n\nExamples:\n\n* A study session counts after it is genuinely completed.\n* A roadmap session counts after the student marks it completed.\n* Assessment statistics update after an assessment is submitted and checked.\n* Study time updates based on actual recorded timer sessions.\n* Streaks update based on real study activity.\n\nC. DATA STORAGE\n\n* Save genuine progress using the existing persistence system.\n* Restore saved progress after refreshing the website.\n* Do not reset existing real student data.\n* Do not overwrite real progress with sample data.\n* Keep different students\' data separate where user accounts are supported.\n\nD. SAMPLE DATA\n\nIf sample or demonstration data is necessary for development, keep it separate from real student accounts.\n\nDo not show sample data in the normal student dashboard.\n\n## 4. FINAL TESTING\n\nAfter making these changes, test:\n\nTEST 1:\nAsk the AI Tutor to explain Newton\'s Second Law. Check whether the answer appears progressively and without unnecessary delays.\n\nTEST 2:\nAsk it to explain the formation of water. Check that the chemical equation is properly formatted.\n\nTEST 3:\nAsk a question involving fractions, powers and square roots. Confirm that the mathematical formatting works.\n\nTEST 4:\nOpen a new student account or a clean dashboard state. Confirm that every progress statistic begins at zero.\n\nTEST 5:\nComplete an actual Pomodoro session and a roadmap session. Confirm that genuine progress is recorded correctly.\n\nTEST 6:\nRefresh the website and confirm that real saved progress remains available.\n\nIMPORTANT FINAL INSTRUCTIONS:\n\n* Preserve all four main features: AI Tutor, Study Roadmap, Paper Analyzer and Focus Tools.\n* Preserve existing saved conversations and genuine student data.\n* Do not replace working features with static mockups.\n* Do not introduce fake responses, fake progress or decorative buttons.\n* Make actual code changes and verify the results.\n* Prioritise reliability, speed, accuracy and a clean user experience."
