  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing API Endpoint Behavior**\n\nI\'m now diving deep into the `/api/tutor/chat` endpoint\'s behavior. The `server.ts` file currently employs `generateContentWithSmartRetry`, utilizing the `gemini-3.8-flash` model. This is where I\'ll begin to optimize and refine the request flow.\n\n\n**Optimizing Chat Context**\n\nI\'ve zeroed in on the context window issue in `/api/tutor/chat`. The current implementation sends the complete conversation history from `AITutorView.tsx`. This is inefficient, as sending 20-30 messages significantly increases processing time. I\'m going to trim the `messages` array, keeping only the last 6 messages (3 user + 3 assistant turns), including subject topic, recent attachments, and initial prompt.\nI\'m exploring Gemini SDK\'s streaming capabilities via Server-Sent Events to optimize latency.\n\n\n**Implementing Streaming Logic**\n\nI\'m now fully immersed in implementing the streaming functionality within `/chat/stream`. I\'ve set up the necessary headers for SSE and structured the data flow using `res.write` to send chunks. I\'m carefully parsing suggestions, quiz data, and handling error events to maintain a robust system. I am maintaining the `/api/tutor/chat` endpoint for backward compatibility. I\'ve also incorporated checks to prevent duplicate requests and implemented `AbortController` for clean stream cancellation. I\'m planning to handle 429 errors within `generateContentWithSmartRetry`, preventing quota exhaustion.\n\n\n**Prioritizing Error Handling**\n\nI\'m now focusing on directly addressing 429 errors from the API by throwing them immediately. This ensures we don\'t waste precious processing time on a blocked request, or risk depleting the quota. I\'m prioritizing this enhancement to improve the application\'s robustness.\n\n\n**Refining LaTeX Rendering**\n\nI\'m now completely focused on enhancing how we display mathematical content. I\'m prioritizing proper LaTeX rendering of fractions, powers, square roots, Greek letters, and various symbols in the output. I\'m focusing on handling edge cases, specifically unescaped characters or raw LaTeX commands like `\\longrightarrow`, `\\rightleftharpoons`, and `\\cdot`. I\'m going to integrate KaTeX with error handling, and implement a fallback to convert raw LaTeX to unicode. My main goal is to ensure students will never see raw, unrendered LaTeX. I am also planning to explain any symbol meanings whenever needed, and create responsive and clean card spacing for it.\n\n\n**Examining KaTeX Integration**\n\nI\'m now investigating how `katex.renderToString` handles errors when streaming. Specifically, I\'m looking at using `{ throwOnError: false }` to prevent crashes when rendering incomplete equations. The goal is to obtain the rendered HTML, even with issues, and handle them gracefully on the client-side. I\'ve been focusing on the impact of this on the dashboard.\n\nI am also examining the `src/services/storage.ts` file, and noticed the dashboard has fake, pre-populated data. I am working to remove the use of this fake data.\n\n\n**Removing Fake Data**\n\nI\'m now fully focused on removing all the hardcoded and artificial data in `ProgressDashboardView.tsx`. This includes completed sessions, study hours, streaks, assessment scores, roadmaps, learning statistics, and progress percentages. I\'m replacing them with a clear, zero-state for new students, ensuring that progress is only tracked based on real user actions. I\'m prioritizing the display of friendly empty-state messages for a clean start.\n\n\n"
