  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Observing Page Rendering**\n\nI\'m currently observing the page\'s render process. Specifically, I\'m noting how the components are structured, particularly the use of `flexbox` for layout, the `min-w-0` and `min-h-screen` attributes, and the conditional rendering of the `AITutor` component based on `currentSection`. I\'m paying attention to how the `header` and `main` elements are arranged.\n\n\n**Analyzing Scroll Behavior**\n\nI\'ve been analyzing the scroll behavior in the `AITutorView` component, focusing on the `h-[calc(100vh-5rem)]` style. This height calculation is giving me pause. I\'m considering what happens on smaller screens or when content overflows. The reported issue of the input box appearing at the top has led me to analyze the scroll context, especially when messages become longer than the viewport. My goal is to determine how to keep the input visible at the bottom and the messages independently scrollable.\n\n\n**Refining Chat Layout**\n\nI\'m now refining the chat layout, focusing on the `App.tsx` and `AITutorView.tsx` components. Specifically, I\'m examining how the parent `main` element controls the scroll behavior. I\'ve realized that the `h-full overflow-hidden flex flex-col` classes are critical when `currentSection` is set to \'tutor\'. Without these, the page body scrolls, leading to undesired behavior in the chat panel and affecting the message composer\'s positioning. My immediate aim is to establish a proper flex-column chat layout.\n\n\n**Examining Height Constraints**\n\nI\'m now carefully examining the height constraints using `100dvh` and `100vh` within the flex layout, specifically in `col md:flex-row h-[calc(100dvh-5.5rem)]`. I\'ve realized the critical importance of `min-h-0` on flex children, particularly the chat panel and message area. Without this, the layout breaks down, allowing children to expand beyond the viewport and triggering undesirable scrolling behavior, leading to the input box misbehaving or displacing. I am now trying to establish consistent height and scroll behavior.\n\n\n**Addressing Scroll Behavior**\n\nI\'ve realized a critical flaw in the scroll handling of the message container. The existing implementation uses `scrollIntoView` unconditionally, which violates the requirement to allow users to read older messages without interruption. The new strategy involves carefully examining the message container\'s `min-h-0` property within the flex layout to enable proper internal scrolling. I\'m focusing on ensuring new messages are auto-scrolled to but avoiding disrupting the user\'s reading.\n\n\n**Implementing Scroll Controls**\n\nI\'m now implementing more refined scroll controls to prevent interruptions. I\'m introducing `isAtBottom` or `userScrolledUp` state tracking using a ref. Now, I will auto-scroll to the bottom only if the user is near the container\'s bottom. I\'ll avoid forcing scroll when the user is reading previous messages and provide a \"Jump to latest\" button when new content is present.\n\nI\'m also re-evaluating the `disabled` state on the input. It seems this was directly blocking the user\'s ability to prepare a response while the AI generated. I\'ll need to remove that to meet requirements.\n\n\n**Adjusting Input Fields**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the input field and send button functionality. I\'ve realized disabling the input during loading is undesirable; the student should be able to type freely. I\'m changing to a `<textarea>` to support multiline text entry, with a placeholder and auto-resizing. I\'m keeping the Send button next to the input, handling the `disabled` state appropriately.\n\n\n**Verifying Flex Layout**\n\nI\'m now verifying the flex layout, specifically ensuring that the Send button stays next to the multiline textarea, as required. I\'ve confirmed they\'re arranged side-by-side using `flex-1` and `shrink-0` classes, respectively. I am also verifying that the input box never covers the last message in the conversation. The layout is achieved with a true flex-column setup, using a flexible chat history container.\n\n\n**Confirming Layout Stability**\n\nI\'m now confirming the stability of the layout and the positioning of the chat input. By examining how the message composer works as a sibling flex item, and observing the chat history `flex-1 min-h-0 overflow-y-auto` behavior, I\'ve confirmed that the input box won\'t cover the last message. Now, I\'m examining how the parent\'s padding in App.tsx influences height.\n\n\n**Refining Layout Constraints**\n\nI\'m now refining layout constraints to prevent unwanted scrolling behavior. I\'ve pinpointed that the padding on the parent `main` element, combined with the header\'s height, causes content to exceed 100vh, triggering window scrolling. To fix it, I\'m adjusting the parent\'s layout and padding, verifying that the `AITutorView` fills the remaining height, and ensuring the input box remains anchored at the bottom. The goal is to eliminate double scrollbars and maintain a stable chat input.\n\n\n"
